&lt;h2 class="heading-element"&gt;🌟 Executive Summary &amp;amp; Problem Statement&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Independent e-commerce makers frequently face the &lt;strong&gt;"Inventory Trap"&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Stockouts&lt;/strong&gt;: Missing out on sudden weekend surges and leaving money on the table.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Dead Stock&lt;/strong&gt;: Over-purchasing slow-moving catalog items and tying up critical working capital.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Traditional time-series forecasting requires extensive statistical tuning (ARIMA stationarity, Prophet holidays, or iterative GBDTs like XGBoost). Meanwhile, attempting to use standard LLMs (like GPT-4) fails due to &lt;strong&gt;token arithmetic hallucinations, linear drift failures, and extreme prompt token costs&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Restock-Predictor&lt;/strong&gt; solves this with a &lt;strong&gt;TabPFN (Prior-Data Fitted Network)&lt;/strong&gt; foundation model — performing zero-shot in-context regression in sub-second inference. Combined with &lt;strong&gt;MongoDB Atlas&lt;/strong&gt; for audit logging and &lt;strong&gt;Streamlit + Plotly&lt;/strong&gt; styled in an obsidian glassmorphism SaaS theme, store owners can upload…&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="gh-btn-container"&gt;&lt;a class="gh-btn" href="https://github.com/sanjana806/Restock-Predictor" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;View on GitHub&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;


&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;GitHub Repository:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://github.com/sanjana806/Restock-Predictor" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;sanjana806/Restock-Predictor&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;License:&lt;/strong&gt; MIT Open Source&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Modular Project Architecture:
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;.streamlit/config.toml&lt;/code&gt;: Custom dark-mode SaaS palette definitions and server configs.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;app.py&lt;/code&gt;: Streamlit frontend featuring custom glassmorphism CSS, Plotly multi-series charts, tabs, and delta metrics.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;predictor.py&lt;/code&gt;: TabPFN API client (&lt;code&gt;tabpfn-client&lt;/code&gt;), time-series tabular feature extraction, and quantile probabilistic forecasting.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;db.py&lt;/code&gt;: MongoDB Atlas connection manager with latency timeouts, ping verification, and resilient session auditing.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;render.yaml&lt;/code&gt;: Infrastructure as Code (IaC) blueprint for one-click deployment on Render.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;sample_data.csv&lt;/code&gt;: 270-row baseline dataset modeled with weekend lifts and realistic trends.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  How I Built It
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  1. The Core AI Model: TabPFN (Prior-Data Fitted Network)
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead of forcing a language model to do math, Restock-Predictor is built around &lt;strong&gt;TabPFN&lt;/strong&gt; (&lt;code&gt;tabpfn-client&lt;/code&gt;), an open-weight foundation model designed purely for tabular data. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;TabPFN was pre-trained on millions of synthetic tabular datasets to approximate Bayesian inference in a single transformer forward pass. It requires:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;No iterative gradient boosting&lt;/strong&gt; (unlike XGBoost or LightGBM).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;No hyperparameter tuning or cross-validation loops&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;No stationarity transformations or ARIMA parameter searching&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h4&gt;


  Tabular Feature Engineering Pipeline (&lt;code&gt;predictor.py&lt;/code&gt;)
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To feed TabPFN the cleanest tabular signal, we extract structural temporal features from the raw sales timestamps:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# Extract tabular representations for TabPFN's in-context attention mechanism
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;df&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;day_index&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;df&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;min_date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;dt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;days&lt;/span&gt;        &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# Continuous linear drift
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;df&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;day_of_week&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;df&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;].&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;dt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;dayofweek&lt;/span&gt;              &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# Weekly seasonality (0=Mon, 6=Sun)
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;df&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;is_weekend&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;df&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;day_of_week&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;].&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;apply&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;lambda&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;5&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;else&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# Weekend Etsy spikes
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;df&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;day_of_month&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;df&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;].&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;dt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;day&lt;/span&gt;                   &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# Payday purchasing patterns
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;df&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;rolling_7d_mean&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;df&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Quantity_Sold&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;].&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;rolling&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;7&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;min_periods&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;mean&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# Current demand baseline
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;df&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;lag_7&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;df&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Quantity_Sold&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;].&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;shift&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;7&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;fillna&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(...)&lt;/span&gt;   &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# Same-day previous week anchor
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h4&gt;


  Probabilistic Quantile Forecasting
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Inventory forecasting isn't just about the average—it's about the risk tail. We query TabPFN for both median expectations and &lt;strong&gt;90th-percentile quantiles&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# Predict both expected demand (median) and 90% risk ceiling for safety stock
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;quantiles&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;model&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;predict&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;X_future&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;output_type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;quantiles&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;quantiles&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mf"&gt;0.5&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;0.9&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;])&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;expected_demand&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;quantiles&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[:,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;safety_stock_ceiling&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;quantiles&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[:,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


  2. Frontend &amp;amp; Visual Polish (Streamlit + Plotly)
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We established a bespoke aesthetic by pairing &lt;code&gt;.streamlit/config.toml&lt;/code&gt; with custom CSS injected via &lt;code&gt;st.markdown&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Transparent dark glassmorphic cards with subtle &lt;code&gt;1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08)&lt;/code&gt; borders.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Interactive Plotly figures with custom tooltips, unified crosshairs, and shaded confidence ribbons (&lt;code&gt;fill="tonexty"&lt;/code&gt;).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Tabbed layout organizing views into Forecasts, Purchase Orders, SKU Trends, and MongoDB Audit Telemetry.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  3. Cloud Audit Trails (MongoDB Atlas)
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every upload triggers a non-blocking insert into MongoDB Atlas via &lt;code&gt;pymongo&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;log_doc&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;timestamp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;datetime&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;now&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;timezone&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;utc&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;isoformat&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(),&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;filename&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;filename&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;row_count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;total_rows&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;item_count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;item_count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;items&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;unique_items&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;metadata&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;metadata_payload&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;collection&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;insert_one&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;log_doc&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;If network restrictions or unconfigured credentials arise during demo testing, a resilient session fallback catches the exception so the UI never crashes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  4. Continuous Deployment on Render
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Configured via &lt;code&gt;render.yaml&lt;/code&gt; with automated pip package resolution and Streamlit port binding to Render's dynamic &lt;code&gt;$PORT&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  Why Does Open Innovation Matter?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Why build with an open-weight tabular foundation model like TabPFN instead of calling a closed-source general LLM like GPT-4 or Claude?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  1. Continuous Numerical Precision vs. Token Hallucinations
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Generative LLMs represent numbers as discrete Byte-Pair Encoding (BPE) tokens. They have no innate understanding of continuous mathematics, often hallucinating sums, failing to extrapolate gradual linear declines, or inventing phantom sales spikes. TabPFN is a specialized transformer trained on synthetic prior mathematical functions, treating numerical values with mathematical continuity.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  2. Sub-Second Bayesian Inference (&amp;lt; 500ms)
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Generating 30 days of multi-item forecasts through an autoregressive text LLM requires generating hundreds of output tokens sequentially, taking 8 to 15 seconds. TabPFN processes the entire feature matrix in a single forward pass in &lt;strong&gt;less than 500 milliseconds&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  3. Native Probabilistic Uncertainty
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Inventory planners cannot rely on deterministic point estimates alone; they need to know the volatility spread to calculate safety stock. Closed LLMs can only provide hand-wavy descriptions ("sales might surge on weekends"). TabPFN outputs true mathematical predictive distributions and quantiles.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  4. Data Privacy &amp;amp; Zero Token Inflation
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Feeding 90 days of multi-item sales logs into a commercial chat API consumes tens of thousands of tokens per run and exposes confidential boutique business metrics to third-party commercial training pools. TabPFN accepts compact numerical arrays locally or via lightweight API endpoints without token bloat.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  My Agent Session
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This entire project was engineered collaboratively with &lt;strong&gt;Antigravity&lt;/strong&gt;, an agentic pair programmer. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  What We Tackled:
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Infrastructure as Code:&lt;/strong&gt; Architecting &lt;code&gt;render.yaml&lt;/code&gt; to overcome common Streamlit PaaS deployment traps (binding to &lt;code&gt;0.0.0.0&lt;/code&gt;, dynamic &lt;code&gt;$PORT&lt;/code&gt; handling, and disabling CORS/XSRF for Render).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Tabular Feature Engineering:&lt;/strong&gt; Designing the rolling window and lag feature transformer in &lt;code&gt;predictor.py&lt;/code&gt; to maximize TabPFN's in-context attention.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Resilient Architecture:&lt;/strong&gt; Writing &lt;code&gt;db.py&lt;/code&gt; with tight socket timeouts and graceful session fallbacks so the app works flawlessly out of the box even before external credentials are plugged in.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;UI/UX Elevation:&lt;/strong&gt; Transforming a default Streamlit layout into a glassmorphic dark SaaS interface complete with interactive Plotly confidence bands and styled gradient dataframes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  My First User
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To test Restock-Predictor with a real-world side hustle, I shared the live Render deployment with &lt;strong&gt;Priya&lt;/strong&gt;, an independent maker running a hand-poured candle and artisan ceramics shop on Etsy.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  The Problem:
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Priya had been ordering candle wax and ceramic clay in arbitrary batches every two weeks. Last month, a sudden weekend feature on an Etsy curation feed caused her best-selling soy candle to sell out in 36 hours. She missed out on an estimated &lt;strong&gt;\$340 in lost sales&lt;/strong&gt; while waiting 5 days for a new shipment of soy wax flakes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  The Test Run:
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Priya exported her last 90 days of sales as a CSV and uploaded it to Restock-Predictor on Render. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Her Feedback:
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;"Seeing the amber 'Safety Buffer' band on the chart made it immediately obvious why I keep running out of stock on weekends. My base sales look manageable, but the weekend surge pushes demand way past my typical reorder point. Having an exact restock number with safety buffer included—and being able to download it as a spreadsheet—saved me hours of manual math."&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Next Feature Request:
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Priya asked if future versions could let her input &lt;strong&gt;lead times for her suppliers&lt;/strong&gt; (e.g., "supplier takes 6 days to deliver") so the app can alert her on the exact calendar day she needs to submit her order.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  Prize Categories
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  🌟 Best Use of Render ($200 Featured Category)
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Restock-Predictor is deployed natively on Render using an Infrastructure as Code blueprint (&lt;a href="https://github.com/sanjana806/Restock-Predictor/blob/main/render.yaml" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;code&gt;render.yaml&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/a&gt;).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Zero-Downtime Web Service:&lt;/strong&gt; Configured with Python 3.11, automatic dependency caching, and clean port forwarding via &lt;code&gt;streamlit run app.py --server.port $PORT --server.address 0.0.0.0&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Production Health Checks:&lt;/strong&gt; Health check polling wired directly to Streamlit's native endpoint &lt;code&gt;/_stcore/health&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Secure Secret Decoupling:&lt;/strong&gt; Database credentials (&lt;code&gt;MONGO_URI&lt;/code&gt;) and model tokens (&lt;code&gt;TABPFN_API_KEY&lt;/code&gt;) are managed through Render's environment dashboard (&lt;code&gt;sync: false&lt;/code&gt;), keeping the public repository fully secure.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  ⚡ Best Use of TabPFN ($200 Featured Category)
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;TabPFN (&lt;code&gt;tabpfn-client&lt;/code&gt;) is the algorithmic core of the platform. We utilize TabPFN's zero-shot in-context learning to perform tabular regression across engineered time-series features without hyperparameter tuning, and extract probabilistic 90th-percentile quantiles to calculate inventory safety stocks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  🍃 Best Use of MongoDB Atlas ($100 Partner Category)
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;All upload batches, row counts, unique catalog SKUs, and processing metadata are immutably logged to a MongoDB Atlas cluster (&lt;code&gt;pymongo&lt;/code&gt;). A dedicated audit tab in the dashboard provides store owners with transparent traceability over past inventory analysis runs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you work with Laravel, you have probably faced this situation at least once.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You get an old database. Maybe it's a &lt;code&gt;.sql&lt;/code&gt; file from a client, or a MySQL schema from a previous project. Now you have to bring it into a Laravel app, and Laravel wants migrations.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So you open the SQL file and start writing &lt;code&gt;Schema::create()&lt;/code&gt; by hand. Table by table. Column by column. &lt;code&gt;VARCHAR(255)&lt;/code&gt; becomes &lt;code&gt;string()&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;BIGINT UNSIGNED&lt;/code&gt; becomes &lt;code&gt;unsignedBigInteger()&lt;/code&gt;, and then you do the same for indexes and foreign keys.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It's boring work, and it's easy to make small mistakes. I got tired of it, so I built a tool for it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Meet the SQL to Laravel Migration Converter
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It's a free online tool on &lt;a href="https://aabitech.com/tools/sql-to-laravel-migration-converter" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AabiTech&lt;/a&gt;. You paste your SQL &lt;code&gt;CREATE TABLE&lt;/code&gt; statements, and it gives you Laravel migration code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No signup, no install. You just open it in your browser and use it.&lt;/p&gt;

